WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:02.520
- You might remember
that in the early 2000's

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:04.710
there was an effort to
rebrand French fries

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:07.860
as freedom fries, a
lexical act of vengeance

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:10.989
for France's opposition to
the US-led invasion of Iraq.

00:00:10.989 --> 00:00:14.880
Not such a sick burn considering
it's probably the Belgians,

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:16.770
not the French, who deserve credit

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:18.750
for America's favorite side dish.

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:20.070
That wasn't the first time

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:22.470
a war provoked a food name change.

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:23.970
During World War I,

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:26.790
German imports like
sauerkraut and frankfurters

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:29.717
were renamed liberty
cabbage and liberty sausage.

00:00:29.717 --> 00:00:32.160
Let's hope we don't go
to war with Italy again,

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:35.550
because I am not calling
a pizza a freedom Frisbee.

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:38.853
Apparently, rebranding
foods over political gripes

00:00:38.853 --> 00:00:42.111
does not work because none
of these monikers stuck.

00:00:42.111 --> 00:00:46.920
But many food etymologies
have even stranger stories.

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:49.440
Some are passed down
over thousands of years,

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:51.690
some invented by marketing wizards,

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:55.680
and some are so mysterious, we
can only guess their origins.

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:59.070
Let's dig to some interesting
histories of food words.

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:01.934
I'm Dr. Erica Brozovsky
and this is "Otherwords."

00:01:01.934 --> 00:01:04.517
(bright music)

00:01:08.991 --> 00:01:10.309
- [Announcer] "Other Words."

00:01:10.309 --> 00:01:12.642
(bright music)

00:01:12.642 --> 00:01:14.005
- The word food comes

00:01:14.005 --> 00:01:18.038
from the Proto-Indo-European
root pa, to feed.

00:01:18.038 --> 00:01:21.993
It's also where we get
pantry, pastor and company.

00:01:21.993 --> 00:01:25.500
And surprisingly, many
of the oldest human foods

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:28.140
have names that are shared
among many languages.

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:30.930
Corn, for example,
originally just referred

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:32.824
to any type of cereal grain,

00:01:32.824 --> 00:01:34.076
and still retains that meaning

00:01:34.076 --> 00:01:36.330
in some English-speaking countries.

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:39.615
In fact, corn and grain
have the same root.

00:01:39.615 --> 00:01:41.100
The native American staple

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:42.989
that the Taino people called mahiz

00:01:42.989 --> 00:01:45.790
was nicknamed Indian corn by Europeans,

00:01:45.790 --> 00:01:49.033
which in the US was eventually
shortened to just corn.

00:01:49.033 --> 00:01:52.170
In the UK, it's known by
the Anglicized version

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:55.680
of a Spanish interpretation
of the Taino word, maize.

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:57.750
Because of their similarity to grains,

00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:00.736
the small berries of the Piper
plant were called peppercorns

00:02:00.736 --> 00:02:03.583
which are dried and ground
to make black pepper.

00:02:03.583 --> 00:02:06.435
This was a very popular
seasoning in the old world,

00:02:06.435 --> 00:02:09.300
reflected by cognates in many languages.

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:12.000
In fact, finding a new
source of peppercorns

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.850
was one of the reasons the
Spanish monarchy bankrolled

00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:17.335
Christopher Columbus's
voyage across the Atlantic.

00:02:17.335 --> 00:02:18.810
What he found instead

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:21.300
were the chilis of
Mexico and the Caribbean.

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:24.150
In what may have been a devious
bit of colonial marketing,

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:27.840
he renamed them peppers, hoping
to cash in on the popularity

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:29.549
of black pepper back home.

00:02:29.549 --> 00:02:32.430
Though they share a name in
a penchant for spiciness,

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:35.368
chili peppers and black pepper
are botanically unrelated.

00:02:35.368 --> 00:02:38.640
Oddly enough, English has
different names for animals

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:41.310
depending on whether
they're alive or dead.

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:43.380
As we mentioned in a previous episode,

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:45.445
this is thanks to the
French-speaking Normans

00:02:45.445 --> 00:02:47.989
who conquered England in the 11th century.

00:02:47.989 --> 00:02:50.190
When English farmers raised livestock,

00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:52.604
they called them pigs, cows, and sheep.

00:02:52.604 --> 00:02:55.650
But when the French nobles
ordered them served on a platter,

00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:58.080
they called it porc, beof and moton.

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:00.180
Venison, the word for deer meat,

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:02.310
came from the old French venesoun,

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:04.789
which roughly meant "the
meat that was just hunted."

00:03:04.789 --> 00:03:06.900
A lot of dish names are accidentally

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:09.656
or deliberately misleading
about what's in them.

00:03:09.656 --> 00:03:12.510
Sweetbreads, for instance,
are not bread at all,

00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:17.040
but cooked glands, usually
from calves or lambs.

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:20.072
The bread part may come
from the old English brǣd,

00:03:20.072 --> 00:03:22.112
meaning flesh or meat.

00:03:22.112 --> 00:03:24.300
Head cheese is not a cheese,

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:27.560
but a loaf of meat made
from a cow or pig's head

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:30.935
minced up and packed
together with gelatin.

00:03:30.935 --> 00:03:34.230
And Rocky Mountain
oysters are not oysters,

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:37.824
but the deep fried testicles
of bulls, pigs or sheep.

00:03:37.824 --> 00:03:40.800
After hearing those three, you
might be frightened to know

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:42.958
what's really in Welsh rabbit,

00:03:42.958 --> 00:03:46.059
but it's actually just
melted cheese on toast.

00:03:46.059 --> 00:03:47.760
Some say the name was created

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:49.530
by the British to be an insult.

00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:51.300
Wales was considered to be so poor

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:53.640
that their rabbit had no rabbit in it.

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:56.790
More recently, capitalism
has forced the food industry

00:03:56.790 --> 00:03:58.246
to be very deliberate about

00:03:58.246 --> 00:04:01.450
what they call their products
to attract the most customers.

00:04:01.450 --> 00:04:04.761
For example, kiwis are
actually native to China,

00:04:04.761 --> 00:04:06.330
but when they were first imported

00:04:06.330 --> 00:04:08.670
to New Zealand in the early 20th century,

00:04:08.670 --> 00:04:11.700
the sellers knew that míhóutáo and qíyìguô

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:14.880
wouldn't roll easily off
English speakers' tongues.

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:16.050
Since they were from China

00:04:16.050 --> 00:04:17.790
and tasted a bit like gooseberries,

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:19.680
they named them, drum roll, please.

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:20.580
Da-da-da.

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:23.231
Chinese gooseberries.
(triumphant music)

00:04:23.231 --> 00:04:24.900
But when Chinese gooseberries

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:27.720
finally made it to America 50 years later,

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:29.520
they didn't sell so well.

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:31.429
And the importers blamed the name.

00:04:31.429 --> 00:04:33.480
First, they tried melonettes,

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:35.502
since they looked kind
of like little melons,

00:04:35.502 --> 00:04:38.430
and then finally settled on kiwifruits,

00:04:38.430 --> 00:04:41.036
named after the adorable
fuzzy birds of New Zealand.

00:04:41.036 --> 00:04:42.810
Third time's the charm.

00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:44.160
The fruits took off,

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:46.470
and the name was eventually
shortened to kiwis.

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:48.798
The ocean is full of lots
of weird-looking creatures

00:04:48.798 --> 00:04:50.823
with unappetizing names.

00:04:50.823 --> 00:04:53.206
If you're a fisherman
who catches slimeheads,

00:04:53.206 --> 00:04:56.610
toothfish, rockfish or lizardfish,

00:04:56.610 --> 00:04:57.810
it might help your sales

00:04:57.810 --> 00:04:59.970
to use a different name on the menu.

00:04:59.970 --> 00:05:02.850
How about orange roughy, Chilean sea bass,

00:05:02.850 --> 00:05:05.233
Pacific snapper, or even Bombay duck?

00:05:05.233 --> 00:05:08.580
And since the name dolphinfish might make people worry

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:10.487
that they're about to
chow down on Flipper,

00:05:10.487 --> 00:05:13.050
you can call it mahi mahi instead,

00:05:13.050 --> 00:05:15.300
a Hawaiian term for "very strong."

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:18.690
Last but definitely not least, sea urchin,

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:20.502
a delicacy known as uni in Japan

00:05:20.502 --> 00:05:22.857
were once known as whore's eggs.

00:05:22.857 --> 00:05:25.500
No one knows why, but I
think we can all agree

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:27.298
sea urchin is a big improvement.

00:05:27.298 --> 00:05:31.590
In 2000, the FDA approved
the California Prune Board's

00:05:31.590 --> 00:05:34.800
request to rebrand the
prune to the dried plum.

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:35.640
Why?

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:37.620
Because of their digestive benefits,

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:39.090
prunes had come to be associated

00:05:39.090 --> 00:05:41.280
with elderly people and bowel problems,

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:43.800
two things advertisers
don't like to mention

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:45.421
when they're marketing
towards the cool kids.

00:05:45.421 --> 00:05:46.460
Did it work?

00:05:46.460 --> 00:05:48.720
Sales improved almost immediately,

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:50.635
especially among younger demographics.

00:05:50.635 --> 00:05:53.407
Prunes were finally having
their moment in the sun.

00:05:53.407 --> 00:05:55.262
Well, I guess they always did.

00:05:55.262 --> 00:05:58.890
Recently, they decided to switch
back to the original name,

00:05:58.890 --> 00:06:00.660
likely because health trends have changed,

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:02.790
and today, having regular bowel movements

00:06:02.790 --> 00:06:04.531
is, like, totally cool, dude.

00:06:04.531 --> 00:06:06.683
(upbeat music)

00:06:06.683 --> 00:06:09.959
Two of the most popular
culinary imports from Mexico,

00:06:09.959 --> 00:06:11.580
tacos and burritos,

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:14.190
have names whose origins
remain mysterious.

00:06:14.190 --> 00:06:16.710
The original meaning
of a Spanish word taco

00:06:16.710 --> 00:06:18.690
is a plug or a stopper,

00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:21.146
perhaps related to the English word tack.

00:06:21.146 --> 00:06:23.790
Some propose that Mexican silver minors

00:06:23.790 --> 00:06:26.575
borrowed the term from the
plugs of explosives they used.

00:06:26.575 --> 00:06:28.816
Gun powder wrapped in a paper tortilla.

00:06:28.816 --> 00:06:30.990
(exploding)
Though tacos can be explosive

00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:33.024
depending on how many
jalapenos are in them,

00:06:33.024 --> 00:06:34.740
some have pointed out

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:37.140
that a mahiz flatbread
stuffed with filling

00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:40.110
probably predates gun
powder in the Americas.

00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:42.000
So it's more likely that the word came

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:45.330
from the Nahuatl word tlahco,
which meant in the middle,

00:06:45.330 --> 00:06:48.420
because you put the filling
in the middle of the tortilla.

00:06:48.420 --> 00:06:51.150
Burrito means little donkey in Spanish,

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:54.204
but it's not clear why that
term became applied to food.

00:06:54.204 --> 00:06:55.689
It might be because burritos are filled

00:06:55.689 --> 00:06:59.130
with so many different
things like a donkey's cargo,

00:06:59.130 --> 00:07:01.830
or maybe it's a reference
to the shape of the bundles.

00:07:01.830 --> 00:07:04.170
One very specific theory suggests

00:07:04.170 --> 00:07:06.510
that during the Mexican Revolutionary War,

00:07:06.510 --> 00:07:08.460
the inventor of the
burrito would sell them

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:10.440
from a little donkey-pulled cart.

00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:11.940
Like many food etymologies,

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:14.424
we'll have to take this
one with a grain of salt.

00:07:14.424 --> 00:07:16.214
From sweet breads to kiwis,

00:07:16.214 --> 00:07:18.588
there's a clear trend
of dressing up our food

00:07:18.588 --> 00:07:21.180
with names that are
attractive and exciting,

00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:22.380
which makes sense,

00:07:22.380 --> 00:07:25.390
because food is such a
pleasurable, communal experience.

00:07:25.390 --> 00:07:27.810
It shouldn't matter
what our food is called

00:07:27.810 --> 00:07:29.670
as long as it tastes good,

00:07:29.670 --> 00:07:30.990
but no one's gonna stay for dinner

00:07:30.990 --> 00:07:33.379
if you're serving
slimehead in prune sauce.

00:07:33.379 --> 00:07:35.647
Maybe Shakespeare was right when he said,

00:07:35.647 --> 00:07:38.670
"A rose by any other name
would smell a sweet."

00:07:38.670 --> 00:07:41.258
But under a different
name, people might not stop

00:07:41.258 --> 00:07:43.299
and smell the thornblotches.

00:07:43.299 --> 00:07:45.930
Need more tasty food shows in your life?

00:07:45.930 --> 00:07:48.187
Head over to the PBS Food YouTube channel

00:07:48.187 --> 00:07:50.160
to check out "Relish."

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:52.830
It's hosted by the
delightful chef Yia Vang,

00:07:52.830 --> 00:07:55.350
who explores different cultures
through our favorite dishes

00:07:55.350 --> 00:07:57.450
in the universal language of food.

00:07:57.450 --> 00:08:00.092
From dumplings and tortas
to grandma's pot roast,

00:08:00.092 --> 00:08:02.550
this show is going to make you hungry.

00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:05.160
You probably shouldn't watch
it on an empty stomach.

00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:06.990
Check out "Relish" on PBS Food

00:08:06.990 --> 00:08:08.970
in the link in the description.

00:08:08.970 --> 00:08:11.520
Finding a new source of pepper
corns was one of the reasons

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:12.927
the Spanish monarchy--

00:08:14.420 --> 00:08:15.690
Bankrolled.

00:08:15.690 --> 00:08:16.631
What the--

00:08:16.631 --> 00:08:19.063
But the bread part may come

00:08:19.063 --> 00:08:21.455
from the old English broad--

00:08:21.455 --> 00:08:22.470
Bread?

00:08:22.470 --> 00:08:23.303
Brede.

